 because the upper limit is finite."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b47b25aca22745d5807c9aed","time_limit":25},{"id":"finite_series_expansion_008_curated20261027_004","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $\\sum_{i=1}^{5}a_i=7$, what is $\\sum_{i=1}^{5}(2a_i+3)$?","options":["$29$","$17$","$20$","$35$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fb79dbaa54d0f23cab41d10a","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_finite_series_4bc5fe3d_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Real numbers $a_1,\\ldots,a_6$ have partial sums defined by $S_0=0$ and $S_k=S_{k-1}+a_k$ for $k=1,\\ldots,6$. It is known that $S_k=k^2$ for $k=0,1,\\ldots,6$. What is $a_5$?","options":["$25$","$11$","$9$","$41$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d94116b663496d46edbb6bcd","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_finite_series_4bc5fe3d_topup20261028_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Real numbers $a_1,a_2,b_1,b_2$ satisfy $a_1+a_2=3$ and $b_1+b_2=4$. What can be concluded about $\\sum_{i=1}^{2}a_ib_i$?","options":["It equals $12$ for every such choice.","It equals $7$ for every such choice.","It can vary, but it never exceeds $12$.","It is not fixed by these two conditions."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9135f58aa26edd301d1cbb60","time_limit":30}]}}